s-strategies-for-flow-control-and-resilience-cover.svg"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"microservices","name":"Microservices","slug":"microservices"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"resilience-engineering","name":"Resilience Engineering","slug":"resilience-engineering"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"apache-kafka","name":"Apache Kafka","slug":"apache-kafka"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"rate-limiting","name":"Rate Limiting","slug":"rate-limiting"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"system-design-distributed-systems","name":"System Design & Distributed Systems","slug":"system-design-distributed-systems"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"system-design","name":"system-design","slug":"system-design"}],"series":null},{"__typename":"Post","id":"adapting-to-virtual-threads-spring-developers","title":"Adapting to Virtual Threads for Spring Developers","subtitle":"Migrate your Spring Boot services from thread-pool exhaustion to JDK 21 virtual threads — the practical Spring developer guide","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/adapting-to-virtual-threads-spring-developers","publishedAt":"2026-04-05T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-09T02:50:29.366Z","slug":"adapting-to-virtual-threads-spring-developers","brief":"Platform threads (one OS thread per request) max out at a few hundred concurrent I/O-bound requests. Virtual threads (JDK 21+) allow millions — with zero I/O-blocking cost. Spring Boot 3.2 enables them with a single property. Avoid `synchronized` blocks wrapping I/O (they pin virtual threads to OS threads) and CPU-bound work inside virtual threads. For I/O-heavy Spring services, virtual threads are the most impactful JVM upgrade since Java 8 lambdas.","readTimeInMinutes":18,"views":107,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/adapting-to-virtual-threads-spring-developers-cover.png"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"java","name":"Java","slug":"java"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"spring-boot","name":"spring-boot","slug":"spring-boot"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"virtual-threads","name":"virtual threads","slug":"virtual-threads"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"project-loom","name":"#project-loom ","slug":"project-loom"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"concurrency","name":"concurrency","slug":"concurrency"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"java-21","name":"java-21","slug":"java-21"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"spring","name":"Spring","slug":"spring"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"performance","name":"performance","slug":"performance"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"software-engineering-principles","name":"software-engineering-principles","slug":"software-engineering-principles"}},{"__typename":"Post","id":"acid-properties-in-sql-databases","title":"ACID Properties Explained: How SQL Databases Guarantee Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability","subtitle":"A deep dive into PostgreSQL WAL logs, MVCC snapshot isolation, Write Skew anomalies, and crash recovery","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/acid-properties-in-sql-databases","publishedAt":"2026-04-17T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-23T04:55:54.302Z","slug":"acid-properties-in-sql-databases","brief":"Trace how storage engines process high-stakes financial transactions like bank transfers using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC), and SSI locks without data loss or corruption.","readTimeInMinutes":38,"views":28,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/acid-properties-in-sql-databases-cover.png"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"databases","name":"Databases","slug":"databases"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"acid","name":"acid","slug":"acid"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"transactions","name":"transactions","slug":"transactions"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"sql","name":"SQL","slug":"sql"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"postgresql","name":"PostgreSQL","slug":"postgresql"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"mysql","name":"MySQL","slug":"mysql"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"how-it-works-internals-explained","name":"how-it-works-internals-explained","slug":"how-it-works-internals-explained"}},{"__typename":"Post","id":"acid-transactions-dynamodb-cosmosdb-spanner","title":"ACID Transactions in Distributed Databases: DynamoDB, Cosmos DB, and Spanner Compared","subtitle":"How modern NoSQL databases achieve atomicity and isolation without a single machine","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/acid-transactions-dynamodb-cosmosdb-spanner","publishedAt":"2026-04-05T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-09T02:50:25.968Z","slug":"acid-transactions-dynamodb-cosmosdb-spanner","brief":"ACID transactions in distributed databases are not equal. DynamoDB provides multi-item atomicity scoped to 25 items using two-phase commit with a coordinator item, but only within a single region. Cosmos DB wraps partition-scoped operations inside a stored procedure using a JavaScript continuation model — cross-partition writes get no atomic guarantee. Google Spanner is the only cloud database that delivers true global ACID with external consistency, using TrueTime (GPS + atomic clocks) to assign globally ordered timestamps and a commit-wait delay to guarantee causality across datacenters. MongoDB supports multi-document transactions on replica sets but carries measurable performance overhead and does not prevent write skew under snapshot isolation. The right choice depends on atomicity scope, latency tolerance, and operational cost — not on which database claims to be \"ACID-compliant.\"","readTimeInMinutes":39,"views":40,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/acid-transactions-dynamodb-cosmosdb-spanner-cover.png"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"distributed-systems","name":"distributed systems","slug":"distributed-systems"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"dynamodb","name":"DynamoDB","slug":"dynamodb"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"cosmosdb","name":"CosmosDB","slug":"cosmosdb"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"spanner","name":"Spanner","slug":"spanner"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"acid","name":"acid","slug":"acid"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"transactions","name":"transactions","slug":"transactions"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"nosql","name":"NoSQL","slug":"nosql"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"how-it-works-internals-explained","name":"how-it-works-internals-explained","slug":"how-it-works-internals-explained"}},{"__typename":"Post","id":"advanced-ai-agents-rag-and-the-future-of-intelligence","title":"Advanced AI: Agents, RAG, and the Future of Intelligence","subtitle":"Is an LLM a brain in a jar? To make it truly useful, we need to give it access to the world. This guide explains RAG and Agents.","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/advanced-ai-agents-rag-and-the-future-of-intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-02-08T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-09T02:50:32.666Z","slug":"advanced-ai-agents-rag-and-the-future-of-intelligence","brief":"Large Language Models are brilliant \"brains in a jar.\" Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) hands them a constantly refreshed memory, while AI Agents give them tools to act in the world. Combined, they turn static knowledge into dynamic, goal-directed intelligence — the next frontier of practical AI.","readTimeInMinutes":15,"views":185,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/advanced-ai-agents-rag-and-the-future-of-intelligence-cover.png"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"advanced-ai","name":"Advanced AI","slug":"advanced-ai"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"ai","name":"AI","slug":"ai"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"llm-engineering","name":"llm-engineering","slug":"llm-engineering"}},{"__typename":"Post","id":"ai-architecture-patterns-routing-planning-memory-and-evaluation","title":"AI Architecture Patterns: Routers, Planner-Worker Loops, Memory Layers, and Evaluation Guardrails","subtitle":"Production AI needs explicit routing, memory, execution, and evaluation layers rather than one loop.","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/ai-architecture-patterns-routing-planning-memory-and-evaluation","publishedAt":"2026-03-13T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-09T02:50:42.723Z","slug":"ai-architecture-patterns-routing-planning-memory-and-evaluation","brief":"A single agent loop is enough for a demo, but production AI systems need explicit layers for routing, execution, memory, and evaluation. Those layers determine safety, latency, cost, and traceability far more than model choice alone.","readTimeInMinutes":14,"views":27,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/ai-architecture-patterns-routing-planning-memory-and-evaluation-cover.svg"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"ai-agents","name":"ai agents","slug":"ai-agents"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"ai-architecture","name":"AI Architecture","slug":"ai-architecture"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"architecture","name":"architecture","slug":"architecture"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"evaluation","name":"Evaluation","slug":"evaluation"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"llmops","name":"#llmops","slug":"llmops"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"system-design","name":"System Design","slug":"system-design"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"llm-engineering","name":"llm-engineering","slug":"llm-engineering"}},{"__typename":"Post","id":"advanced-graph-algorithms-for-interviews","title":"Advanced Graph Algorithms: Dijkstra, Topological Sort, and Disjoint Set Union (DSU)","subtitle":"Master shortest paths, dependency resolution, and cycle detection with robust Java solutions.","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/advanced-graph-algorithms-for-interviews","publishedAt":"2026-06-18T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-09T02:50:36.126Z","slug":"advanced-graph-algorithms-for-interviews","brief":"Graph algorithms are the backbone of networking, scheduling, and connection analysis. This guide explores Dijkstra's pathfinding, Topological Sorting for dependency resolution, and Disjoint Set Union (DSU) for cycle detection, complete with runnable Java code.","readTimeInMinutes":12,"views":8,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/advanced-graph-algorithms-for-interviews-cover.svg"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"java","name":"Java","slug":"java"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"dsa","name":"DSA","slug":"dsa"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"graph-algorithms","name":"graph algorithms","slug":"graph-algorithms"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"coding-interviews","name":"coding interviews","slug":"coding-interviews"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"datastructures-and-algorithms","name":"datastructures-and-algorithms","slug":"datastructures-and-algorithms"}},{"__typename":"Post","id":"ai-agents-explained-when-llms-start-using-tools","title":"AI Agents Explained: When LLMs Start Using Tools","subtitle":"An LLM can talk, but an AI Agent can *act*. We explain how Agents use the ReAct framework to brow...","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/ai-agents-explained-when-llms-start-using-tools","publishedAt":"2026-03-09T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-09T02:50:39.434Z","slug":"ai-agents-explained-when-llms-start-using-tools","brief":"A standard LLM is a brain in a jar — it can reason but cannot act. An **AI Agent** connects that brain to tools (web search, code execution, APIs). Instead of just answering a question, an agent executes a loop of Thought → Action → Observation until the goal is reached.","readTimeInMinutes":13,"views":35,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/ai-agents-explained-when-llms-start-using-tools-cover.png"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"ai","name":"AI","slug":"ai"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"ai-agents","name":"ai agents","slug":"ai-agents"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"autonomous-agents","name":"autonomous agents","slug":"autonomous-agents"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"generative-ai","name":"generative ai","slug":"generative-ai"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"llm","name":"llm","slug":"llm"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"react-framework","name":"react framework","slug":"react-framework"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"llm-engineering","name":"llm-engineering","slug":"llm-engineering"}},{"__typename":"Post","id":"ann-index-types-when-to-choose-hnsw-ivf-pq-flat","title":"ANN Index Types Explained: When to Choose Flat, HNSW, IVF, or IVF-PQ","subtitle":"A production-first guide to picking the right vector index for recall, latency, memory, and update patterns.","url":"https://abstractalgorithms.dev/ann-index-types-when-to-choose-hnsw-ivf-pq-flat","publishedAt":"2026-05-30T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-10-09T02:50:49.779Z","slug":"ann-index-types-when-to-choose-hnsw-ivf-pq-flat","brief":"If your dataset is small and correctness is critical, use Flat. If you need high recall with low latency and enough RAM, use HNSW. If your corpus is huge and memory is your bottleneck, use IVF-PQ. Start with HNSW for most RAG systems, then move to IVF or IVF-PQ when scale and cost force compression.","readTimeInMinutes":14,"views":143,"author":{"__typename":"User","name":"Abstract Algorithms","profilePicture":"https://cdn.hashnode.com/res/hashnode/image/upload/v1770487824632/88a54ce1-0b80-4d20-baf4-1da06ccf4c7a.jpeg"},"coverImage":{"__typename":"CoverImage","url":"/assets/articles/ann-index-types-when-to-choose-hnsw-ivf-pq-flat-cover.svg"},"tags":[{"__typename":"Tag","id":"ann","name":"ANN","slug":"ann"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"vector-database","name":"vector database","slug":"vector-database"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"rag","name":"RAG ","slug":"rag"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"llm","name":"llm","slug":"llm"},{"__typename":"Tag","id":"system-design","name":"System Design","slug":"system-design"}],"series":{"__typename":"Series","id":"llm-engineering","name":"llm-engineering","slug":"llm-engineering"}}],"journey":{"slug":"system-design-and-distributed-systems","label":"System Design & Distributed Systems","description":"Learn System Design & Distributed Systems as a connected topic across chapters, concepts, simulations, and interview reasoning.","domain":"general","concepts":["System Design","System Design & Distributed Systems","Apache Kafka","Distributed Computing","Flow Control","Message Queues","Microservices","Rate Limiting","Resilience Engineering","System Resilience"],"articles":[{"id":"backpressure-preventing-overload-in-distributed-streams","title":"Backpressure: Preventing Overload in Distributed Streams","slug":"backpressure-preventing-overload-in-distributed-streams","brief":"How do distributed systems prevent slower components from being overwhelmed by faster ones, ensuring stability?","readTimeInMinutes":2,"tags":[{"name":"Flow Control","slug":"flow-control"},{"name":"Distributed Computing","slug":"distributed-computing"},{"name":"System Resilience","slug":"system-resilience"},{"name":"Message Queues","slug":"mes